{"title":"1977 AHSME Problems/Problem 30","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/d/c5d372c564b6b059dc5b124a1c926be386b9ff26.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] for (int i=0; i&lt;9; ++i) { draw(dir(10+40*i)--dir(50+40*i)); } draw(dir(50) -- dir(90)); label(&quot;$a$&quot;, dir(50) -- dir(90), N); draw(dir(10) -- dir(90)); label(&quot;$b$&quot;, dir(10) -- dir(90), SW); draw(dir(-70) -- dir(90)); label(&quot;$d$&quot;, dir(-70) -- dir(90), E); //Credit to MSTang for the diagram [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/b/e9bdf3e496517e8f8742f81dd774be1f93cf6e8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are the lengths of a side, a shortest diagonal and a longest diagonal, respectively, \nof a regular nonagon (see adjoining figure), then\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/0/73036ecf54d60d013bd4b8c4004dbcf80405f9d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }d=a+b\\qquad \\textbf{(B) }d^2=a^2+b^2\\qquad \\textbf{(C) }d^2=a^2+ab+b^2\\qquad\\\\ \\textbf{(D) }b=\\frac{a+d}{2}\\qquad  \\textbf{(E) }b^2=ad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"475\" height=\"58\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By the law of cosines we can get the following expressions for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/2/362a0180bf7706f185d9ca4e111734b59f125130.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d^{2}$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/9/929daabdabe6092db3cac67b44d8de517f087ef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b^{2}$\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/c/04ccb0543eabcd1eaf705a4eb9f4cfcc2bf92c6a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d^{2}=2b^{2}(1-\\cos (100^\\circ))\\]\" width=\"182\" height=\"19\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/9/24934de07dfa7a4b8f9cd48546b0a70ae6bfc8e9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[b^{2}=2a^{2}(1-\\cos (140^\\circ))\\]\" width=\"182\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>We can substitute what we got for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/d/a1d03c3c5b9abf1bdb0c9df06fe5fb11ae13d0e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" /> into the expression for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/2/362a0180bf7706f185d9ca4e111734b59f125130.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d^{2}$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/9/30917f6297ddf283002ff8f629d5555dac1687b2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d^{2}=4a^{2}(1-\\cos (140^\\circ))(1-\\cos (100^\\circ))=4a^{2}(1-(\\cos (100^\\circ)+\\cos (140^\\circ))+\\cos (100^\\circ)\\cos (140^\\circ))\\]\" width=\"713\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>Now apply sum-to-product and product-to-sum identities:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/7/00765a1db8736058079e8dfb23c6e4afdc7ba5a3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d^{2}=4a^{2}(1-(2\\cos (120^\\circ)\\cos (20^\\circ))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (240^\\circ)+\\cos (40^\\circ)))\\]\" width=\"487\" height=\"37\" />\nSimplifying further gives us: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/8/fa8fc5f7329ccb140ce2360be739c471eceb20e1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d^{2}=4a^{2}(1+\\cos (20^\\circ)-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (40^\\circ))\\]\" width=\"313\" height=\"37\" />\nAfter using the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/d/2bdabce604514ec842c95388a4bd35f1364b14bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos (40^\\circ)=2\\cos^2 (20^\\circ)-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"202\" height=\"19\" />, it's not hard to see that the expression in the parentheses is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/4/bf454c43703d33cef71e187d367f828d54d0ffe9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\cos (20^\\circ)+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"119\" height=\"37\" />. So we can square-root both sides to find the expression for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/0/dc09b194d5fb3eec408d4ab98c5e59c6dc33b07b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d=2a(\\cos (20^\\circ)+\\frac{1}{2})\\]\" width=\"164\" height=\"37\" />\nNow let's look at the expression for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/d/a1d03c3c5b9abf1bdb0c9df06fe5fb11ae13d0e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" />. We can apply the reverse of the double angle identity to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/7/aa7b087b0eff9b2aa5e3d824c05104e6b02f615a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-\\cos (140^\\circ)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"107\" height=\"20\" /> equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/1/6915a9b128371ec947643bd197de7a579adf62e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sin^2 (70^\\circ))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"20\" />. So if we square root the entire expression we get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/f/a7f1e561f968bf57f681f84200d9296d27ebb266.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[b=2a\\sin (70^\\circ)=2a\\cos (20^\\circ)\\]\" width=\"224\" height=\"18\" />\nWe now have everything in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Luckily when we consider choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/9/ea92a40d2ca37df23434cf3b86e05bc9f19a4619.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> we can verify without much work that this must be the answer.\n</p><p>Solution by harita19\n</p>\n<ul><li>NOTE: a much easier solution exists by drawing some lines and recognizing that the nonagon is cyclic, but for those of use who use algebra in every geometry problem, this is the best solution.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Using Van Schooten's Theorem, we draw an equilateral triangle in the nonagon to see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/b/52b9170e6bb4a955d2a14eb981c9f1c64b18a5a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b=d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Reflect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> such that it connects the left vertex of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />nd vertex counterclockwise. This length is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />. Notice that when connecting the two endpoints of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> segments, we achieve a length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. We now have an isosceles trapezoid with bases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>Next, we can find the angle between the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> segments as follows. The interior angle of a regular nonagon measures <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/f/73fccb25b2113f257773eab2cde640e6052ba453.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180-\\frac{360}{9}=140^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"142\" height=\"38\" />. Thus, the isosceles triangle formed from two of the side lengths of the nonagon and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> has angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/5/0b51850ccd3440214d4ccfbc14d29b2a31bfda55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$140$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/a/b7adc89f0765dbb3fdf68f0739ee6b246ba36dd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{180-140}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"83\" height=\"38\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b9cf501e52afb703cd6e339c496206d47bf4208.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{180-140}{2}=20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"125\" height=\"38\" />. Then, the angle between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/d/29d2fccd35be5bd8671ee6c12aef740251c192e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$140-20=120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"121\" height=\"15\" />. We can therefore deduce that the other angles are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/a/aaa4de95cc6371d125d5cf773cdd9f9913dd1d8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/302e99c1c7cd91a6ae371139e5142ccef5977dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/302e99c1c7cd91a6ae371139e5142ccef5977dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> degrees.\n</p><p>We can drop a perpendicular from both endpoints of segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> to segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to get a rectangle and two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651ba418a810d971dbc8a326d49a71fe1541fae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/302e99c1c7cd91a6ae371139e5142ccef5977dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/c/bfcfee7bd8a5e15e5f6b7881c138de43651572a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> triangles. Each of the triangles has a shorter leg length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/6/c16ac7a0bf4ae8f820ff6642106958ef347582b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{d-a}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"47\" height=\"39\" />, and hence, we can deduce that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/2/472667aa4ac7202876353e194238376fa7012dfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{d-a}{2} \\cdot 2=b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"101\" height=\"39\" />. Hence, the correct answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/0/230688ecd9c1e08df1eba2cb69d9b9b16e368085.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{(A)} \\; d=a+b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"110\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>- Solution by scjh999999\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_AHSME_Problems/Problem_30&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Draw chords of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to create two triangles as follows.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/2/4e247cac164cbaec6f4345fe421953efce1c6ee9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] for (int i=0; i&lt;9; ++i) { draw(dir(10+40*i)--dir(50+40*i)); } draw(dir(50) -- dir(90)); label(&quot;$a$&quot;, dir(50) -- dir(90), N); draw(dir(-70) -- dir(90)); label(&quot;$d$&quot;, dir(-70) -- dir(90), E); draw(dir(-150) -- dir(50)); label(&quot;$d$&quot;, dir(-150) -- dir(50), W); draw(dir(-150) -- dir(-70)); label(&quot;$b$&quot;, dir(-150) -- dir(-70), S); //diagram based on the one in Solution 1 [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>Because each edge of a nonagon has a central angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/3/bc37a1f655537de0702b4d2a6f62de97db08fe6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />, and inscribed angles are half the corresponding central angle, the two triangles thus created are equilateral.  This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/d/a7deb5e9e3ecc76a6cd52f8e1bd6a47561b32e77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d = a + b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>- Solution by Dr.B.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017050330\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.021 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.028 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 297/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 11450/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:15098:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017050330 and revision id 258001. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}